he 4). My hand is extremely weak, losing to any pocket pair, any card pairing the board (5, 7, J), and any high card better than a 6 (8, 9, 10, Q, K, A). The opponent has checked all streets, which suggests weakness, but their range likely includes many high cards (A-high, K-high) that beat my 6-kicker. Bluffing here is risky because the pot is small, and opponents often call river bets with A-high or bottom pair after checking down. Since I am currently leading in the overall game score, the best strategy is to minimize variance and loss by checking back. If I check, I lose nothing more; if I bluff and get called, I lose chips unnecessarily.\n\nReason: My hand has very little showdown value, only beating missed straight draws containing a 4 or 3. The opponent likely holds high cards that beat me. Bluffing is high-risk for a small pot. Checking is the safest option to preserve my chip lead.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
